-- Verified access details — batch 11
--
-- 17 spot(s) with records, 37 skipped. Every sourceUrl is an official
-- government, park-authority, council or fisheries-body page (CLAUDE.md content rules 1-3).
-- Apply by hand in the SQL Editor, then rebuild (migration first, then build).
--
-- ruegen-baltic-sea-trout: LALLF M-V (Landesamt fuer Landwirtschaft, Lebensmittelsicherheit und Fischerei, the state fisheries authority) Fischereidokumente page: three documents are always required in Mecklenburg-Vorpommern coastal waters (Fischereischein, Angelerlaubnis and proof of the state Fischereiabgabe); the coastal waters are the Bodden, Haffe, Peenestrom, Achterwasser and the Baltic within German territorial waters; the page states there are no free angling waters in MV; tourist licence valid up to 28 days. Existing regulations ("Fischereischein plus a coastal fishing permit") were right but omitted the Fischereiabgabe; line extended. No ramp or shore-access detail found on an official page.
--
-- sylt-north-sea: Nationalpark Wattenmeer (Schleswig-Holstein Wadden Sea National Park authority) page on recreational angling: angling is generally possible in the coastal strip except in marked breeding and resting sites in salt marshes; boat angling is possible almost everywhere in the park except the usage-free zone south of the Hindenburgdamm. Existing regulations (Fischereischein required; national-park restrictions) are consistent. I did NOT claim that the prohibited boat zone includes Sylt: the page does not say so. Notes-only record.
--
-- sete-mediterranean-tuna: French Ministry of the Sea (mer.gouv.fr) page "Pratiquer la peche de loisir au thon rouge": annual authorisation from the regional maritime directorate (DIRM) is required to fish bluefin tuna recreationally in the East Atlantic and Mediterranean from a French-flagged or EU-registered recreational/charter vessel; third-country-flag vessels are prohibited; a retained tuna must be tagged immediately; landings must be declared to FranceAgriMer within 48 hours. Existing regulations already mention authorisation and tag; this adds the who/how. One-per-vessel figure from the page deliberately not repeated (bag-number rule 3). No port/ramp detail for Sete found on an official page, so notes-only.
--
-- normandy-bass-cotentin: French Ministry of the Sea (mer.gouv.fr) page "Peche de loisir en mer": the page mentions no licence requirement (existing "no licence" line kept); shore anglers must respect minimum sizes and closures, leave no waste on the foreshore and tell someone where they are going; recreational fishers aged 16+ targeting designated sensitive species, including sea bass in the Channel and Bay of Biscay zones, must register annually through the RecFishing app and declare catches daily; catches are marked by removing the lower part of the tail fin; shellfish sanitary zone maps exist. CONTRADICTION FIXED: existing regulations listed only "no licence" and size/bag limits and missed the registration and marking duties, which a reader could be fined for ignoring; line added. No specific Cotentin ramp or rock-mark access page found (manche.gouv.fr pages errored in the browser), so notes-only.
--
-- sicily-bluefin-tuna: Egadi Islands Marine Protected Area management body (ampisoleegadi.it, run under the Comune di Favignana and the Italian environment ministry), zoning page, read via WebFetch and cross-checked with a search summary of the same page: sport fishing is listed only for zones B and C and is subject to authorisation; spearfishing is banned throughout the MPA; zone A is the integral reserve; zone D is the protection zone between the islands. The two summaries differed on whether zone C also needs authorisation, so the record says authorisation applies and points to the MPA body for the procedure. Existing regulations (MASAF registration, RecFishing, bluefin authorisation) are unchanged; the national bluefin nulla-osta detail from the Guardia Costiera was not added because it is a different page.
--
-- canary-islands-marlin: Gobierno de Canarias fisheries department page on recreational maritime fishing licences, read via WebFetch (Spanish): three licence classes (class 1 surface trolling from vessels, class 2 spearfishing, class 3 surface fishing from land or vessels without trolling), valid three years, issued through the regional electronic office; marlins, tunas, swordfish and hake are "species under differentiated protection" that need a separate authorisation requested from the national Ministry of Agriculture, which keeps management and control. Existing regulations (licence, billfish released, authorisations may apply) consistent; the class and the national authorisation route are the additions.
--
-- costa-brava-spinning: Generalitat de Catalunya, Parcs Naturals page "Activitats a mar" for the Cap de Creus Natural Park (Girona coast), summarised via WebFetch: recreational fishing is allowed in the marine part of the park with a special park permit obtained after training, in addition to the general licence; daily catch reporting and marking; only catch-and-release in the coves of Murtra, Caials and Tamariua; fishing banned in the S'Encalladora integral reserve. Two separate summaries of the park rules disagreed about the partial reserves, so nothing is stated about them. The spot description was not checked against Cap de Creus: the record is written as applying to fishing INSIDE the park and says so. CONTRADICTION FIXED: existing regulations said marine protected areas along this coast are "no-take", which is too broad (the park allows permitted fishing outside its integral reserve); reworded to "some".
--
-- hardangerfjord-sea-trout: HEALTH ADVISORY. Same Mattilsynet page as oslofjord-sea-trout: for Hardangerfjord/Sorfjorden (contaminated with cadmium, lead, mercury, dioxins and PCB) it lists several restrictions: avoid brosme (tusk) and pollock in specified zones, pregnant and nursing women to avoid crab, lobster and stationary fish in the inner areas, and shellfish restrictions in designated zones. The page does not list sea trout, so nothing is claimed about it, and the exact zones were not read, so the record points to the page for them. Advisory-only record; no access page found.
--
-- oslofjord-sea-trout: HEALTH ADVISORY. Mattilsynet (Norwegian Food Safety Authority) page "Unngaa fisk og skalldyr fra forurensede havner, fjorder og innsjoer", read via WebFetch: for inner Oslofjord (inside Drobak) it advises pregnant and nursing women and children under 5 not to eat cod fillet because of mercury, and the general population to avoid mussels from the inner harbour area because of PAH. The page does not list sea trout, so no claim is made about it. No access/ramp page found; this is an advisory-only record, which is the useful part.
--
-- vattern-char: HEALTH ADVISORY. Livsmedelsverket (Swedish Food Agency) page "Fisk som kan innehalla miljogifter", read in a real browser, last reviewed 2025-09-23: wild-caught char (roding) from Vattern, and wild trout, whitefish and salmon from Vattern, can contain high levels of dioxin and PCB; children, young people, people who are pregnant, breastfeeding or planning pregnancy should eat them at most 2-3 times a year, others can eat them about once a week; eel should not be eaten at all. Existing regulations unchanged; advisory-only record (no ramp/parking detail from an official page).
--
-- bohuslan-coast-mackerel: Lansstyrelsen Vastra Gotaland (county administrative board) "Fiske" page, read via WebFetch (Swedish): sea fishing with hand-held gear is free; from 1 January to 31 March fishing for cod is prohibited inside the trawl boundary; fishing for cod is prohibited all year in the Gullmarsfjorden protection area; from 1 October to 31 March fishing is prohibited in all protected areas (fredningsomraden); maps are on Svenska Fiskeregler. The page's size minimum for cod is deliberately NOT repeated (content rule 3). Existing regulations (free unlicensed rod fishing, cod restricted, sea trout closures near river mouths) consistent; the dated closures are the additions.
--
-- vindelalven-grayling: Lansstyrelsen Vasterbotten (county administrative board) page "Ansok om fiske i kvoterade vatten", read via WebFetch (Swedish): Vindelalven from Mankeforsen to Sarngejukkes is quota-managed; permits are allocated by lottery, applications open 1 January to 30 April with results from the second half of May; up to five fishing days can be applied for on the Vindelalven; fly fishing with a fly rod only on most quota waters; catch reporting is mandatory; the season is 1 July to 20 August on the Vindelalven. Catch-number and size-window details on the page are deliberately NOT repeated (content rule 3). CONTRADICTION FIXED: existing regulations said only "a local fiskekort is required", which would send an angler to buy a card for a stretch where a lottery permit is needed.
--
-- gotland-sea-trout: Two official sources. (1) Lansstyrelsen Gotland (county administrative board) "Fiske" page, read via WebFetch: sea trout protected 1 Sept-31 Jan in 25 protected areas at 34 watercourses, from 2025 also within the watercourses themselves; pike and perch protected 1 Mar-31 May in coastal waters out to 4 nautical miles around all of Gotland; free handheld-tackle fishing in coastal waters. (2) Livsmedelsverket (Swedish Food Agency) advice page, read in a real browser: wild trout from the Baltic can contain high dioxin and PCB levels (HEALTH ADVISORY). CONTRADICTION/GAP FIXED: existing regulations mentioned only river-mouth protection zones and sea trout size; the pike/perch coastal closure was missing and the protection now extends into the watercourses. The record carries one sourceUrl (the county board), so the Food Agency advisory (Baltic trout) is NOT written into the record; a follow-up could add it once the record shape allows a second source.
--
-- inari-lake-trout: Metsahallitus (Finnish state land manager) Eraluvat permit-shop page for angling permit area 1504 Inarijarvi, read via WebFetch: covers Lake Inarijarvi and Lake Nitsijarvi; the angling permit is specific to the boating party (one valid permit per boat); the number of permits is limited; fisheries management fee applies to lure anglers aged 18-69; hook-and-line and ice fishing with one rod are free. Prices deliberately not stated. Existing regulations (management fee plus regional permit) consistent; the regional fishing-area rules were not read.
--
-- kymijoki-river: Metsahallitus Eraluvat page for angling permit area 7581 Langinkoski (Kymijoki, Kotka), read via WebFetch: lowest rapids of the Langinkoski branch inside the Langinkoski nature reserve near Kotka centre; fly fishing only; hook-and-line and ice fishing prohibited; maximum eight anglers at once; native fish (adipose fin intact) must not be kept; season 1 Jan-30 Sept and 1-31 Dec; permit plus fisheries management fee. The spot is described only as the River Kymijoki, so the record is written as "at Langinkoski" and says so. Existing regulations (beat permit, catch-and-release, hydro releases) consistent.
--
-- ruunaa-rapids: Metsahallitus Eraluvat page for permit area 7525 Ruunaan kosket (Finnish), read via WebFetch: stretch from Paasikoski to below Siikakoski including Murroojarvi and Kattilajarvi; allowed methods; wading prohibited 1 Sept-31 May; fishing banned in the Paasikoski-Mutikaisenkari section 1 Sept-30 Nov; Kattilakoski run as a reserved rapid for group bookings; rod permit plus fisheries management fee; open all year. The page title for the permit was summarised as a free rod permit, so the record says "rod-fishing permit" only. Existing regulations consistent; the seasonal no-wading and closed-section dates are the additions.
--
-- vuoksi-river-trout: City of Imatra (local authority) page "Vuoksen kalastusalueet ja lupaehdot", read via WebFetch: Vuoksi (excluding Mellonlahti) and Mellonlahti need different permits; fly fishing, spinning and trolling allowed; bait and ice fishing allowed without a Vuoksi permit; motor trolling restricted to the stretch Kyyronkoski to the border zone; all fishing prohibited 15 Oct-15 Nov between the railway bridge and the river mouth; grayling protected 1 Apr-31 May. The page did not address Saimaa salmon release (already in existing regulations) and did not say where permits are bought, so neither is stated.
--
